  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nWe aim to improve the upper bound for the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem constant by searching for a step function h on [0,2] with integral 1 that minimizes the maximum overlap shift. Our approach combines local optimization and multi-resolution search: starting from the parent solution (47-point step function), we optimize within fixed grids using simulated annealing adapted for combinatorial constraints. This method replaces intermediate states through connected swaps while maintaining integral constraints, enabling exploration of both fine-grained adjustments and shifts to higher-resolution grids when budget allows. The search prioritizes extreme local modifications that naturally satisfy constraints, avoiding computational projection and penalty methods. By systematically examining grid doublings and distributing computation time, we aim to push below the current record 0.3808.\n\nMathematical specification:\nThe objective is to minimize C = max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx for step functions h with 0\u2264h\u22641 and \u222bh=1. In discrete form on n-point grids, we represent h as a probability vector satisfying \u2211h_i = n/2 (for integral 1). The transform is C = (2/n) * max(np.correlate(h,1-h))\n(mode='full'). The local moves are swaps between grid indices, preserving the integral.\n\nImplementation procedure:\n1. Input: parent solution(s) for defined grid sizes, time budget, random seed\n2. Parent validation: check parent satisfies n/2 integral and bounds \n3. Grid initialization:\n   - Start with grid size 47 (n=47) from parent if available\n   - Double grid size (n \u2248 94, 188, ...) until time permits\n4. State: probability vector h of length current grid size with sum(n/2)\n5. Overlap computation: `subtractive_correlation_score(h, grid_size)`\n6. Mutation: swap 2 random elements while maintaining sum\n7. Simulated annealing:\n   - T = T_initial * \u03b1^steps, cooling until T_min\n   - Accept improvements unconditionally\n   - Accept worse solutions with exp(-\u0394/T)\n   - Stop after time or temperature threshold\n8. Restart at every grid boundary\n9. Maintain a parallel best-so-far structure across grids\n10. Output: best solution, its C, grid size\n\nSearch choices and variations:\n- Base configuration:\n  - T_initial = 100, \u03b1=0.99, T_min=0.01\n  - Population sizing: n=47,94,188,... \u2248 budget/ (n * log n)\n  - Move structure: random swaps of two indices\n- Expansion rules:\n  - If best overlap below target in a grid, double grid\n  - Ensure distinct sweeps at each resolution\n- Abandonance criteria:\n  - No improvement in 5 steps per grid at a certain temperature\n  - Time precision: allocate budget before each grid change\n- Mode properties:\n  - C hypersensitive to grid size: higher resolution may \n    reveal structure breaking current records\n\nComputational budget and robustness:\n- Dominant costs:\n  - Grid size n: O(n^2) for correlation, O(n) per evaluation\n  - Total operations directly dependent on n^2 * log(1/T_min)\n- Feasible schedule: allocate 60% time to best grid, 30% to neighbors, 10% cooling\n- Termination checks:\n  - Deadlock: temperature cools too rapidly\n  - Radius collapse: small moves without improvement\n  - Manual grid progression after 10^5 evaluations\n- Best fallback: highest-quality parent in any grid\n- Response to issues:\n  - Label invalid moves\n  - Maintain integral during all operations\n  - Use clipping for off-boundary projections\n\nValidation and return contract:\n- Pre-compute overlap using provided correlation method \n- Ensure 0\u2264h_i\u22641, \u2211h=n/2 in all states\n- Return solution with minimal computed C\n- Test cross-validation on samples for sanity checks\n- Record the grid size used for final solution\n- Conclude only upon time exhaustion unless explicit improvement",
